1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,758
An oil company drills exploration wells one after another. Each

2
00:00:03,858 --> 00:00:08,230
well strikes oil with probability twenty percent, independently. How many

3
00:00:08,330 --> 00:00:11,170
wells will it take to get the first strike? This

4
00:00:11,270 --> 00:00:14,110
time we don't fix the number of trials; we wait.

5
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:19,857
Two distributions describe waiting: the geometric, for the first

6
00:00:19,957 --> 00:00:22,735
success, and the negative binomial, for the r-th.

7
00:00:23,833 --> 00:00:26,954
For the first strike to come on the fourth well, the

8
00:00:27,054 --> 00:00:30,299
first three must be dry and the fourth must hit: point

9
00:00:30,399 --> 00:00:34,077
eight, times point eight, times point eight, times point two.

10
00:00:35,366 --> 00:00:38,959
In general, the chance the first success comes on well y is q

11
00:00:39,059 --> 00:00:42,470
to the y minus one, times p. Each bar is eighty percent of

12
00:00:42,570 --> 00:00:46,707
the one before it, so the bars decay geometrically, which is where the

13
00:00:46,807 --> 00:00:50,339
name comes from. On average you wait one over p wells: five.

14
00:00:51,433 --> 00:00:55,298
Now a subtle point. Suppose the first five wells have all been

15
00:00:55,398 --> 00:00:58,752
dry. Is a strike now due? Many people feel it must be.

16
00:00:59,350 --> 00:01:02,947
Throw away the first five bars, and stretch what's left so

17
00:01:03,047 --> 00:01:06,708
it adds up to one again. That's conditioning. And look: the

18
00:01:06,808 --> 00:01:11,489
stretched bars are exactly the original distribution, just shifted along by

19
00:01:11,589 --> 00:01:15,442
five. The process has no memory. After any number of failures,

20
00:01:15,542 --> 00:01:18,502
the wait ahead looks exactly like a fresh start.

21
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:21,642
In symbols: the chance of waiting b more, given

22
00:01:21,742 --> 00:01:24,717
you've already waited a, is just the chance of

23
00:01:24,817 --> 00:01:28,661
waiting b. For independent trials, luck doesn't accumulate.

24
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:34,442
What if the company needs three successful wells, not one? Then it

25
00:01:34,542 --> 00:01:38,344
waits for the third success. The last well must be a strike,

26
00:01:38,444 --> 00:01:42,636
and among the earlier wells exactly two are strikes, in any order.

27
00:01:42,933 --> 00:01:46,767
That gives the negative binomial. Its shape is a smoother

28
00:01:46,867 --> 00:01:49,942
hump, and its mean is r over p, fifteen wells.

29
00:01:50,042 --> 00:01:54,014
That makes sense: waiting for three successes is like three

30
00:01:54,114 --> 00:01:57,879
geometric waits, one after another, each averaging five.

31
00:01:59,966 --> 00:02:05,401
So: the geometric counts the wait for a first success, with mean one over

32
00:02:05,501 --> 00:02:10,405
p. It has no memory, so a success is never 'due'. And the negative

33
00:02:10,505 --> 00:02:15,409
binomial counts the wait for the r-th success, with mean r over p.

